assachusetts-tax-calculator#breadcrumb\\\",\\\"@type\\\":\\\"BreadcrumbList\\\",\\\"itemListElement\\\":[{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"ListItem\\\",\\\"position\\\":1,\\\"name\\\":\\\"Home\\\",\\\"item\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com\\\"},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"ListItem\\\",\\\"position\\\":2,\\\"name\\\":\\\"Massachusetts Tax Calculator\\\"}]},{\\\"@id\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/massachusetts-tax-calculator#webapp\\\",\\\"@type\\\":\\\"WebApplication\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"Massachusetts Tax Calculator 2026\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/massachusetts-tax-calculator\\\",\\\"applicationCategory\\\":\\\"FinanceApplication\\\",\\\"operatingSystem\\\":\\\"Web\\\",\\\"offers\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Offer\\\",\\\"price\\\":0,\\\"priceCurrency\\\":\\\"USD\\\",\\\"availability\\\":\\\"https://schema.org/InStock\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/massachusetts-tax-calculator\\\"},\\\"author\\\":{\\\"@id\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/massachusetts-tax-calculator#author\\\"},\\\"publisher\\\":{\\\"@id\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/#organization\\\"}},{\\\"@id\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/massachusetts-tax-calculator#author\\\",\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Person\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"Rachel Mitchell\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/about#rachel-mitchell\\\",\\\"jobTitle\\\":\\\"Lead Tax Analyst \\u0026 Editorial Director, CPA\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Rachel Mitchell is a Certified Public Accountant (CPA) licensed in Illinois with over 12 years of experience in individual and small-business taxation. She specializes in federal and state income tax compliance, FICA optimization, payroll tax strategy, and multi-state tax planning. Rachel holds an MS in Taxation from Golden Gate University and a BS in Accounting from the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign. She is an active member of the American Institute of Certified Public Accountants (AICPA) and the Illinois CPA Society. Before joining TheTaxCalc, Rachel spent 8 years at a Big Four accounting firm advising high-net-worth clients on tax-efficient wealth strategies.\\\",\\\"knowsAbout\\\":[\\\"Federal Income Tax\\\",\\\"State Income Tax\\\",\\\"FICA Tax\\\",\\\"Payroll Tax\\\",\\\"IRS Compliance\\\",\\\"Tax Planning\\\",\\\"Multi-State Taxation\\\",\\\"Tax-Advantaged Accounts\\\"],\\\"sameAs\\\":[\\\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/rachelmitchellcpa/\\\",\\\"https://www.aicpa-cima.com/\\\"],\\\"worksFor\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Organization\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"TheTaxCalc\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com\\\"},\\\"image\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/author-rachel-mitchell.webp\\\"},{\\\"@id\\\":\\\"https://thetaxcalc.com/massachusetts-tax-calculator#faq\\\",\\\"@type\\\":\\\"FAQPage\\\",\\\"mainEntity\\\":[{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Question\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"How much is Massachusetts state income tax in 2026?\\\",\\\"acceptedAnswer\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Answer\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Massachusetts has a flat 5% rate on most income, plus a 4% surtax (the millionaire's tax) on income exceeding $1 million — making the effective top rate 9%. For the vast majority of earners making under $1M, it's just 5%. That's lower than many progressive states' top rates but higher than flat-tax states like Pennsylvania (3.07%) or Indiana (3.05%).\\\"}},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Question\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"Does Massachusetts have a standard deduction?\\\",\\\"acceptedAnswer\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Answer\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Nope. Massachusetts doesn't offer a standard deduction or personal exemption at the state level. Your entire gross income (minus any pre-tax deductions like 401(k) or HSA) is subject to the 5% rate. That's a drawback compared to states like Georgia or North Carolina that offer standard deductions.\\\"}},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Question\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"What is the Massachusetts millionaire's tax?\\\",\\\"acceptedAnswer\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Answer\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Voters approved a 4% surtax on income above $1 million starting in 2023, bringing the effective rate to 9% on that portion. So if you earn $1.2M, the first $1M is taxed at 5% ($50,000) and the next $200K is taxed at 9% ($18,000). It only affects roughly 1% of Massachusetts taxpayers but generates significant revenue for education and transportation.\\\"}},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Question\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"Does Massachusetts tax Social Security benefits?\\\",\\\"acceptedAnswer\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Answer\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"No. Massachusetts does not tax Social Security benefits. However, most other retirement income — including 401(k) withdrawals, IRA distributions, and pensions — IS taxed at the 5% flat rate. That makes Massachusetts less retiree-friendly than states like Florida or Texas that have no income tax at all.\\\"}},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Question\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"How does the overall tax burden in Massachusetts compare?\\\",\\\"acceptedAnswer\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Answer\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Massachusetts has a moderate-to-high overall tax burden. The 5% flat income tax is reasonable, but property taxes average 1.15%, the combined sales tax is 6.25% (state only — no local sales tax), and there's no standard deduction. On a $75K salary, you'd pay about $3,750 in state income tax, which is more than Indiana ($2,220 after exemptions) but less than California (around $4,100).\\\"}},{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Question\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"Is Massachusetts tax-friendly for retirees?\\\",\\\"acceptedAnswer\\\":{\\\"@type\\\":\\\"Answer\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Not particularly. While Social Security is exempt, pensions, 401(k), and IRA withdrawals are all taxed at 5%. Property taxes are above average at 1.15%, and the cost of living in Massachusetts — especially housing near Boston — is among the highest in the country. Florida or New Hampshire (no income tax on wages) are better options for retirees in the region.\\\"}}]}]}\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"15:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 py-8 sm:px-6 lg:px-8\\\",\\\"children\\